data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If the `work` delegate is long-running and must cooperate

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

Callers then check cancellation inside their work:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the work won't stop unless it checks the token. CPU-bound loops must call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at regular intervals. |
| **Fire-and-forget leak** | The first example (no token in `work`) means the delegate keeps running after timeout — you've abandoned it, not killed it. Only use this if the work is short or you accept the resource cost. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers. Always `using`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removal** | This has no equivalent — remove it entirely. Cooperative cancellation doesn't inject exceptions, so there's nothing to reset. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (here). Don't swallow it deeper in the call chain. |

### What was removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Deleted — not needed |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |

### Critical Difference

**Old model (preemptive):** Runtime forcibly injects an exception into the target thread at any point.  
**New model (cooperative):** The work must *voluntarily* check for cancellation. If it doesn't, it runs to completion even after timeout — you can only abandon it, not kill it.

For truly uncooperative code (e.g., third-party library with no cancellation support), the only option is to run it in a **separate process** and call `Process.Kill()`.
